h different rebel groups controlling  different parts and different  countries allied to those different  rebel groups  if we were able to reunify the congo we  will be able to do anything else  joseph kabila actually brought that war  to an end in 2003 negotiating a  settlement with all the various  countries that were involved  soon he was eventually elected president  in 2006 and he took over a country that  is one of the richest countries in the  world in terms of natural resources  but its population is one of the poorest  joseph kabila was looking to rebuild the  country rebuild the economy and he  needed cash  he tried to open up  congo's vast and world-class reserves of  copper cobalt gold to international  investors  at the beginning there were plenty of  western companies who showed interests  in investing in and developing these  assets but over time their interest  has dwindled and they've departed partly  because of the unpredictability of  congolese politics partly because of  the reputation of  for corruption in congo  but this isn't to say that there are not  major  western companies there in fact the  biggest producer of cobalt it may well  be the biggest producer of copper  is glencore which is headquartered in  switzerland  however in recent years the trajectory  has been  more and more chinese companies coming  in congo has a very very significant uh  mining industry it's africa's biggest  producer of copper it's by far the  world's biggest producer of cobalt and  say the last 10 to 15 years chinese  companies making the running piling in  with investments  so they've gone from a pretty bit part  player to  the largest producers of both metals in  congo  the deal that really turbo charged the  involvement of chinese mining companies  in the congolese mining sector happened  in 2008  and according to this deal  chinese companies would build three  billion dollars of infrastructure and  also invest an additional 3.2 billion in  a huge copper cobalt